window.MTH105_INDEX=[{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"From data to decisions","x":"Statistics is the science of collecting, organizing, analyzing, interpreting and communicating data to support decisions. Topics: Descriptive and inferential statistics · Definitions of representative samples and importance of sample size, and impact of outliers · Illustrative practical examples of use of Statistics. CLO 1."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s1","n":"Descriptive vs inferential","t":"Descriptive and inferential statistics","x":"Descriptive statistics describe the data we have: tables, graphs, averages. Inferential statistics use a sample to estimate or test something about the whole population."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s2","n":"Population and sample","t":"Population vs sample","x":"The population is the whole group we want to study (size N). A sample is the part we actually measure (size n). Numbers that describe a population are parameters; numbers from a sample are statistics."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s3","n":"Types of data","t":"Types of data","x":"Qualitative data are categories: nominal (no order) or ordinal (ordered). Quantitative data are numbers: discrete (counted) or continuous (measured)."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s4","n":"Sort the variables","t":"Activity · Classify the variables","x":"Pick the type for each variable."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s5","n":"Sample size","t":"Representative samples and sample size","x":"A representative sample looks like the population: choose it at random, not by convenience. Bigger random samples give estimates closer to the truth."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s6","n":"Outliers","t":"The impact of outliers","x":"An outlier is a value far from the rest. It pulls the mean towards it; the median barely moves."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s7","n":"Check the claim","t":"Activity · Is this conclusion valid?","x":"A campus newsletter polls 30 students in the main library at 9 PM: 21 say they prefer studying at night. Headline: “70% of students prefer studying at night.”"},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s8","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"New examples. Answer first, then check."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s9","n":"Exit quiz","t":"Exit quiz","x":"Five quick questions."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s10","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next week: Ch.2, Picturing Variation with Graphs."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"Organize before you analyze","x":"Raw data are hard to read. Tables and graphs show the pattern. Topics: Frequency distributions · Histograms and frequency polygons · Cumulative frequency distributions. CLO 1."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s1","n":"Relative frequency","t":"Frequency and relative frequency","x":"Count each category (f), then divide by the total (n) to get the share."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s2","n":"Bar and pie","t":"Bar charts and pie charts","x":"For categories: a bar chart compares sizes (bars with gaps); a pie chart shows parts of a whole (100%)."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s3","n":"Class width","t":"Grouping numbers into classes","x":"For many numbers, group them into classes of equal width. Width = range ÷ number of classes, rounded up."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s4","n":"Frequency table","t":"Frequency distribution and histogram","x":"Build the table: class, boundaries, midpoint, frequency, relative and cumulative frequency. A histogram draws it with touching bars."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s5","n":"Shape","t":"The shape of a histogram","x":"Follow the tail. Tail to the right: positively skewed. Tail to the left: negatively skewed. No long tail: symmetric."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s6","n":"Polygon and ogive","t":"Frequency polygons and cumulative frequency","x":"A frequency polygon joins the midpoints and closes on the axis. An ogive plots cumulative frequency at each upper boundary."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s7","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"New data. Answer on paper first."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s8","n":"Exit quiz","t":"Exit quiz","x":"Five quick questions."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s9","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next: Weeks 3–4, Ch.3: Numerical Summaries of Center and Variation."},{"pt":"Weeks 3–4","l":"week-03.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"One number for the centre, one for the spread","x":"The same mean can hide very different spreads. We need both. Topics: Mean, median, and mode · Percentiles · Variance and standard deviation · Coefficient of variation. CLO 1."},{"pt":"Weeks 3–4","l":"week-03.html#s1","n":"Mean","t":"The mean","x":"The mean is the balance point: add all values, divide by how many there are."},{"pt":"Weeks 3–4","l":"week-03.html#s2","n":"Median and mode","t":"Median and mode","x":"Median: the middle value after sorting, at position (n + 1) ÷ 2. Mode: the most frequent value, the only centre for nominal data."},{"pt":"Weeks 3–4","l":"week-03.html#s3","n":"Which centre?","t":"Mean, median, mode and skew","x":"Outliers and skew pull the mean towards the tail. For skewed data, report the median."},{"pt":"Weeks 3–4","l":"week-03.html#s4","n":"Quartiles and IQR","t":"Quartiles, range and IQR","x":"Sort the data. The median splits it in two halves; Q1 is the median of the lower half (25th percentile), Q3 of the upper half (75th percentile). Range = max − min. IQR = Q3 − Q1."},{"pt":"Weeks 3–4","l":"week-03.html#s5","n":"Variance","t":"Sample variance","x":"Variance is the average squared distance from the mean. For a sample, divide by n − 1."},{"pt":"Weeks 3–4","l":"week-03.html#s6","n":"Standard deviation","t":"Standard deviation","x":"The standard deviation is the square root of the variance: the typical distance from the mean, in the original units."},{"pt":"Weeks 3–4","l":"week-03.html#s7","n":"CV","t":"Coefficient of variation","x":"The CV is the SD as a percentage of the mean. Use it to compare spread across different means or units."},{"pt":"Weeks 3–4","l":"week-03.html#s8","n":"Check any data","t":"Tool · Check your calculations","x":"Type any data set to check the mean, median, mode, s², s and CV step by step."},{"pt":"Weeks 3–4","l":"week-03.html#s9","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Order the data first. Check with the tool."},{"pt":"Weeks 3–4","l":"week-03.html#s10","n":"Exit quiz","t":"Exit quiz","x":"Five quick questions."},{"pt":"Weeks 3–4","l":"week-03.html#s11","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next: Week 5, Ch 5: Modeling Variation with Probability."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"From describing to predicting","x":"Weeks 1–4 described data we have. Probability measures how likely something is before it happens: a number from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain). Topics: Definitions of events and sample space · Operations on probabilities. CLO 2."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s1","n":"Sample space and events","t":"Experiment, outcome, sample space, event","x":"An experiment has uncertain results. Each result is an outcome. All outcomes form the sample space S. An event is a set of outcomes we care about."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s2","n":"Basic rule","t":"The basic rule","x":"When outcomes are equally likely, count: favourable outcomes over all outcomes. Every probability is between 0 and 1, and all outcomes add up to 1."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s3","n":"Complement","t":"The complement rule","x":"“Not A” is everything outside A. The two always add to 1."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s4","n":"Addition rule","t":"The addition rule and Venn diagrams","x":"“A or B” = at least one happens. Subtract the overlap so it is not counted twice. If A and B are mutually exclusive (no overlap), just add."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s5","n":"Multiplication rule","t":"The multiplication rule and tree diagrams","x":"For independent events, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). In a tree, multiply along a path; add paths for “or”."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s6","n":"Types of events","t":"Activity · Name the relationship","x":"Pick the relationship between the two events."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s7","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Name the rule first, then calculate."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s8","n":"Exit quiz","t":"Exit quiz","x":"Five quick questions."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s9","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next: Weeks 6–7, discrete probability distributions."},{"pt":"Weeks 6–7","l":"week-06.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"Every value, with its probability","x":"A random variable X turns outcomes into numbers. A discrete one takes countable values (0, 1, 2 …). Its distribution lists every value with its probability. Topics: Definition of discrete random variables · Expected value, variance and standard deviation of discrete random variables · Examples of discrete random variables: Bernoulli and Binomial distributions. CLO 2."},{"pt":"Weeks 6–7","l":"week-06.html#s1","n":"Valid distribution","t":"A valid probability distribution","x":"Two conditions: every P(x) is between 0 and 1, and all P(x) add up to 1. A missing value k = 1 − (sum of the others)."},{"pt":"Weeks 6–7","l":"week-06.html#s2","n":"Expected value","t":"Expected value E(X)","x":"E(X) is the long-run average of X: multiply each value by its probability and add."},{"pt":"Weeks 6–7","l":"week-06.html#s3","n":"Variance and SD","t":"Variance and standard deviation of X","x":"Var(X) measures how far X typically lands from E(X). SD = √Var(X)."},{"pt":"Weeks 6–7","l":"week-06.html#s4","n":"Bernoulli","t":"The Bernoulli distribution","x":"One trial, two outcomes: success (x = 1) with probability p, failure (x = 0) with q = 1 − p. E(X) = p and Var(X) = p·q."},{"pt":"Weeks 6–7","l":"week-06.html#s5","n":"Binomial or not?","t":"Activity · Is it binomial?","x":"Binomial needs all four: fixed n, two outcomes, constant p, independent trials."},{"pt":"Weeks 6–7","l":"week-06.html#s6","n":"Binomial formula","t":"The binomial formula","x":"P(X = x): choose which x trials succeed, then multiply the success and failure probabilities."},{"pt":"Weeks 6–7","l":"week-06.html#s7","n":"Mean of binomial","t":"Mean, variance and SD of a binomial","x":"Shortcuts: E(X) = n·p, Var(X) = n·p·q, SD = √(n·p·q)."},{"pt":"Weeks 6–7","l":"week-06.html#s8","n":"Binomial tool","t":"Tool · Binomial probabilities","x":"Move n, p and x. Watch the shape and check your answers."},{"pt":"Weeks 6–7","l":"week-06.html#s9","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Check validity first. Use the tools to check answers."},{"pt":"Weeks 6–7","l":"week-06.html#s10","n":"Exit quiz","t":"Exit quiz","x":"Five quick questions."},{"pt":"Weeks 6–7","l":"week-06.html#s11","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next: Weeks 8–9, the normal distribution."},{"pt":"Weeks 8–9","l":"week-08.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"From counting to measuring","x":"Binomial X was counted (0, 1, 2 …). Height, time and weight are measured: they are continuous. Topics: Usage of Histogram to introduce continuous random variables and their density functions · The normal distribution · The standard normal distribution · Interpreting the standard deviation: Z-scores. CLO 2."},{"pt":"Weeks 8–9","l":"week-08.html#s1","n":"Density curve","t":"From histogram to density curve","x":"With more data and thinner bars, the histogram becomes a smooth density curve. The area under it is 1, and area = probability."},{"pt":"Weeks 8–9","l":"week-08.html#s2","n":"Normal curve","t":"The normal distribution","x":"Bell-shaped and symmetric: mean = median = mode at the peak. μ moves the curve; σ makes it narrow or wide."},{"pt":"Weeks 8–9","l":"week-08.html#s3","n":"Empirical rule","t":"The 68–95–99.7 rule","x":"In a normal distribution about 68% of values lie within 1σ of μ, 95% within 2σ, and 99.7% within 3σ."},{"pt":"Weeks 8–9","l":"week-08.html#s4","n":"z-score","t":"The z-score","x":"A z-score counts how many standard deviations a value is from the mean. Positive: above the mean. Negative: below."},{"pt":"Weeks 8–9","l":"week-08.html#s5","n":"Z-table","t":"Reading the Z-table","x":"The Z-table gives the area to the left of z. Row: first decimal. Column: second decimal."},{"pt":"Weeks 8–9","l":"week-08.html#s6","n":"Find probabilities","t":"Tool · Normal probabilities","x":"Convert to z, look up the table, then subtract when needed."},{"pt":"Weeks 8–9","l":"week-08.html#s7","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Draw the curve and shade the area before you calculate."},{"pt":"Weeks 8–9","l":"week-08.html#s8","n":"Exit quiz","t":"Exit quiz","x":"Five quick questions."},{"pt":"Weeks 8–9","l":"week-08.html#s9","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next: Weeks 10–11, sampling distributions and confidence intervals."},{"pt":"Weeks 10–11","l":"week-10.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"One sample, a range of plausible values","x":"Every sample gives a slightly different mean. Knowing how sample means vary lets us estimate the population mean with a margin of error. Topics: Definitions of sampling distribution of the mean · Confidence intervals of the mean and that of a proportion. CLO 3."},{"pt":"Weeks 10–11","l":"week-10.html#s1","n":"Sampling distribution","t":"The sampling distribution of the mean","x":"Take many samples of size n and plot their means. That is the sampling distribution of x̄. By the Central Limit Theorem, for n ≥ 30 it is approximately normal, even if the population is skewed."},{"pt":"Weeks 10–11","l":"week-10.html#s2","n":"Standard error","t":"Standard error and z for a sample mean","x":"The sample means centre on μ, and their spread is the standard error σ ÷ √n. Bigger n, smaller SE."},{"pt":"Weeks 10–11","l":"week-10.html#s3","n":"z* values","t":"Confidence level and z*","x":"The confidence level sets the multiplier z*."},{"pt":"Weeks 10–11","l":"week-10.html#s4","n":"CI for a mean","t":"Confidence interval for a mean","x":"Interval = x̄ ± margin of error, where E = z* × σ ÷ √n."},{"pt":"Weeks 10–11","l":"week-10.html#s5","n":"CI for a proportion","t":"Confidence interval for a proportion","x":"For a percentage, use p̂ = x ÷ n and its standard error √(p̂(1 − p̂) ÷ n)."},{"pt":"Weeks 10–11","l":"week-10.html#s6","n":"CI tool","t":"Tool · Build an interval","x":"Change n or the confidence level and watch the width."},{"pt":"Weeks 10–11","l":"week-10.html#s7","n":"Interpret","t":"Activity · Say it correctly","x":"The true mean is fixed. The 95% describes the method, not one interval."},{"pt":"Weeks 10–11","l":"week-10.html#s8","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Write the formula, substitute, then use the calculator."},{"pt":"Weeks 10–11","l":"week-10.html#s9","n":"Exit quiz","t":"Exit quiz","x":"Five quick questions."},{"pt":"Weeks 10–11","l":"week-10.html#s10","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next: Week 12, contingency tables and the chi-square test."},{"pt":"Week 12","l":"week-12.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"Are two categories related?","x":"Is exam result related to study method? Both are categorical, so we count and compare. Topics: Tests of independence of the 2 variables using Chi-square test. CLO 4."},{"pt":"Week 12","l":"week-12.html#s1","n":"Five steps","t":"The logic of a hypothesis test","x":"H₀: the variables are independent (not related). H₁: they are related. Assume H₀; reject it only if the data are very unlikely under it."},{"pt":"Week 12","l":"week-12.html#s2","n":"Expected counts","t":"Expected frequencies","x":"If the variables were independent, each cell would get its fair share of the totals."},{"pt":"Week 12","l":"week-12.html#s3","n":"Chi-square","t":"The chi-square statistic","x":"χ² adds up how far each observed count is from its expected count. Big χ²: the data do not fit independence."},{"pt":"Week 12","l":"week-12.html#s4","n":"Decide","t":"Degrees of freedom and decision","x":"df = (rows − 1)(columns − 1). Compare χ² with the critical value. The test is always right-tailed."},{"pt":"Week 12","l":"week-12.html#s5","n":"Chi-square tool","t":"Tool · Test any table","x":"Type observed counts. Add rows or columns. The tool finds E, χ², df and the decision."},{"pt":"Week 12","l":"week-12.html#s6","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Write H₀ and H₁ first."},{"pt":"Week 12","l":"week-12.html#s7","n":"Exit quiz","t":"Exit quiz","x":"Five quick questions."},{"pt":"Week 12","l":"week-12.html#s8","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next: Weeks 13–15, simple linear regression."},{"pt":"Weeks 13–15","l":"week-13.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"Use x to predict y","x":"Regression fits a straight line through a scatter plot so we can predict y from x. Topics: Equation of regression line · Correlation between two variables · Interpretation of Coefficient of Determination and Correlation Coefficient · Interpretation of Regression Coefficients. CLO 5."},{"pt":"Weeks 13–15","l":"week-13.html#s1","n":"x and y","t":"Independent and dependent variables","x":"x is the predictor (independent). y is the outcome we predict (dependent). Decide which is which before you calculate."},{"pt":"Weeks 13–15","l":"week-13.html#s2","n":"Correlation r","t":"Correlation coefficient r","x":"r measures the strength and direction of a straight-line relationship, from −1 to +1."},{"pt":"Weeks 13–15","l":"week-13.html#s3","n":"Read r and r²","t":"Interpreting r and r²","x":"r² is the share of the variation in y explained by x. r = 0.775 → r² ≈ 0.60: 60% of the variation in y is explained by x. Correlation does not prove causation."},{"pt":"Weeks 13–15","l":"week-13.html#s4","n":"Slope and intercept","t":"The regression line ŷ = a + bx","x":"Build a table of x, y, x², xy. Find the slope b first, then the intercept a."},{"pt":"Weeks 13–15","l":"week-13.html#s5","n":"Interpret","t":"Interpreting the coefficients","x":"Slope: each extra unit of x changes ŷ by b. Intercept: ŷ when x = 0, only meaningful if x = 0 makes sense. Do not predict far outside the data (extrapolation)."},{"pt":"Weeks 13–15","l":"week-13.html#s6","n":"Regression tool","t":"Tool · Fit your own line","x":"Type pairs. The tool builds the table, finds b, a, r and r², and predicts."},{"pt":"Weeks 13–15","l":"week-13.html#s7","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Table first: x, y, x², xy, y²."},{"pt":"Weeks 13–15","l":"week-13.html#s8","n":"Exit quiz","t":"Exit quiz","x":"Five quick questions."},{"pt":"Weeks 13–15","l":"week-13.html#s9","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Final exam: comprehensive, more weight on topics after the midterm."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"What the midterm covers","x":"Each topic: a short recap with a tool, then new practice. Try first, then open the answer."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s1","n":"Histogram shape","t":"Shape: follow the tail","x":"The tail names the shape. Tail to the right: positively skewed, mean > median. Tail to the left: negatively skewed, mean < median."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s2","n":"Polygon and ogive","t":"Frequency polygon and ogive","x":"A frequency polygon joins the class midpoints at their frequencies and closes on the axis. An ogive joins cumulative frequencies at the upper class boundaries."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s3","n":"Practice · Graphs","t":"Practice · Graphs","x":"New data. Answer on paper first."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s4","n":"Boxplot","t":"Boxplot: five numbers","x":"Min, Q1, median, Q3, max. Range = max − min. IQR = Q3 − Q1, the middle 50%. Outlier: beyond Q1 − 1.5·IQR or Q3 + 1.5·IQR."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s5","n":"Practice · Boxplot","t":"Practice · Boxplot","x":"Compute one, read one."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s6","n":"Centre and spread","t":"Centre and spread","x":"Centre: mean, median, mode. Spread: s², s and CV = s ÷ x̄ × 100%. Use the CV to compare spread between data sets with different means."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s7","n":"Practice · Spread","t":"Practice · Centre and spread","x":"Use a deviation table. Check your answers with the tool in the previous scene."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s8","n":"Venn diagrams","t":"Venn diagrams and the addition rule","x":"Fill from the middle out: both, then each “only”, then neither. P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). P(neither) = 1 − P(A or B)."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s9","n":"Practice · Venn","t":"Practice · Venn diagrams","x":"This time the overlap is missing. Find it first."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s10","n":"Tree diagrams","t":"Tree diagrams and the multiplication rule","x":"Branches from one point add to 1. Multiply along a path for “and” (independent events). Add paths for “or”."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s11","n":"Practice · Tree","t":"Practice · Tree diagrams","x":"Unequal branches this time."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s12","n":"Discrete distributions","t":"Discrete probability distributions","x":"Valid when every P(x) is between 0 and 1 and ΣP(x) = 1. E(X) = Σx·P(x) is the long-run average. Var(X) = Σ(x − μ)²·P(x), SD = √Var(X)."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s13","n":"Practice · Distribution","t":"Practice · Discrete distributions","x":"Find k first. Every later part depends on it."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s14","n":"Exit quiz","t":"Exit quiz","x":"Seven quick questions across all topics."},{"pt":"Midterm practice","l":"midterm-practice.html#s15","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Good luck on Monday. Bring a standard calculator."}];
